= Using the parameterstring class =

Sometimes a function needs to take an arbitrary set of parameters.  For
example, a function for connecting to a database may need host, port, socket,
username and password, any of which could be omitted depending on the
database.  Though C++ support methods which take an arbitrary number of
parameters, sometimes it is more convenient to for the method to accept a
single string parameter with name/value pairs in it instead.

The [../classes/html/classparameterstring.html parameterstring class] provides methods for parsing and accessing
a parameter string of the following form:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
name1='value1';name2='value2';name3='value3'
}}}
}}}

The single quotes are optional.  If a parameter needs to contain a single
quote, then it can be escaped as follows:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
name='\'value\''
}}}
}}}

Backslashes can be similarly escaped:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
name='\\value\\'
}}}
}}}

The delimiter between parameters defaults to a semicolon, but can be changed using the setDelimiter() method.

The following example parses a parameter string, accesses individual values, clears the string, sets a different delimiter, and parses another string.

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
@../../examples/parameterstring.cpp@
}}}
}}}

